.TH SALT 1 "2026" "salt" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
salt \- cat with colors, faithful to the bytes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B salt
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-c | \-p ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR LANG ]
.RI [ FILE ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B salt
prints files like cat and colors them like bat, under one rule: the
bytes are never changed, only colored. Selecting and copying salted
output yields the exact source \- no line-number gutters, no frames.
When stdout is not a terminal the output is byte-identical to the
input, so salt is always safe in a pipeline.
.PP
Input is processed one line at a time, so
.B tail \-f log | salt \-l yaml
highlights live. Markdown gets rendered emphasis, headers, lists and
links, with fenced code blocks highlighted in their own language \-
while remaining valid Markdown on the clipboard.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Line numbers (opt-in; they do end up in copies).
.TP
.B \-c
Color even when piped (for
.BR "less \-R" ).
.TP
.B \-p
Never color.
.TP
.BI \-l " LANG"
Force the language (name or extension); otherwise detection uses the
file extension, well-known basenames, then the shebang.
.TP
.B \-L
List languages: c, sh/psh, python, markdown, json, diff, yaml, toml,
javascript/typescript, go, rust, ruby, elixir.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 for unreadable or binary files, 2 for usage errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR psh (1),
.BR cat (1)
.SH LORE
You salt a pistachio; salt colors a file. Part of the psh armory. 🫛
